    <node id="n_augustin_jedd_6215e882c99f">
      <data key="d3">Augustin Jedd</data>
      <data key="d4">CHARACTER</data>
      <data key="d5">Dean of St. Marvell's, portrayed by Mr. John Clayton. | Father of Salome and Sheba, whom they plan to coax for money. | Father of Salome and Sheba, referred to as poor Papa. | The father of Miss Jedd, who is out of the way according to Blore. | Portly man of about fifty, father of Salome and Sheba. | A character in the play, father of Salome and Sheba, who invites Tarver and Darbey to dinner and is concerned about bills. | Father of Sheba and Salome, concerned about bills. | A character in the play, father of Sheba and Salome, concerned about household expenses and the Minister Spire restoration. | A clergyman and father of Salome and Sheba, brother of Georgiana Tidman. | Brother of Georgiana Tidman, widower, father of Salome and Sheba, and Dean of St. Marvells. | A character in the play, brother of Georgiana, referred to as Augustin or Gus. | A widower for fifteen years, brother of Georgiana, and host at the Deanery. | A widower of fifteen years, addressed as Jedd and Gus, who holds a position in St. Marvells. | A character in the play, referred to as Jedd, who is the brother of Georgiana and uncle of Salome and Sheba. | A character in the play, referred to as 'pa' by Sheba and Salome, and as 'Augustin' by Georgiana. | Father of Sheba and Salome, from whom they owe forty pounds unknown. | A character who is the father of Salome and Sheba, and has offered a thousand pounds to the Restoration Fund. | A character who has offered a thousand pounds to the Restoration Fund and is anxious for the post to bring today's Times. | A character who is worried about a thousand pounds to the Restoration Fund and bills, and later reads about seven wealthy people contributing. | Brother of Georgiana, a clergyman, concerned about propriety. | Father of Georgiana, Sheba, and Salome; reluctant to house Dandy Dick. | A character in the play who is upset with Mr. Darbey and later enters with Blore carrying a lantern. | A character who is the Dean, referred to as Jedd and Gus, and is unhappy about Sir Tristram staying. | A dean who is short of money and concerned about the church spire. | A clergyman who is horrified by Georgiana's proposal and concerned about the state of the Deanery. | Clergyman who gives Blore fifty pounds to bet on his horse Dandy Dick, despite earlier resisting temptation. | A character in the play who is upset and plans to administer a bolus to a horse in his stable. | The Dean of the Deanery, who is mixing a pudding and reminiscing about his youth. | A character who takes the basin and goes to the stable, wearing Sir Tristram's coat and cap. | The father of Salome and Sheba, mentioned by Sheba. | A person referred to by Blore as having been taken by the constable. | Master of Blore, referred to as the Dean. | A character who appears at the wicket with his head enveloped in a fur cap and is later released from the cell by Hannah. | A dean who is in trouble with the law and hiding from his family and community. | A dean facing a calamity of almost national importance, being taken to Durnstone. | A clergyman who is eating a joint prepared by Hannah. | A gentleman who has been accused by Noah of alienating Hannah's affections and embezzling his meal. | The Dean of St. Marvells, brother of Georgiana, who has mysteriously disappeared. | The Very Reverend Augustin Jedd, Dean of St. Marvells, missing person. | Man in handcuffs, accused of dosing Dandy Dick. | A man discovered administering a remedy to a horse, accused of trespassing and alienating Noah's wife's affections. | The Dean, who is rescued and gradually recovers. | A character in the play who is rescued and is concerned about funding the reward. | A character in the play, referred to as Papa by Sheba, who is concerned about the horse Dandy Dick and the money won towards the Spire. | Brother of Georgiana, who was hauled out of a cart by Sir Tristram and had a nose-bag put over his head. | Father of Salome and Sheba, referred to as Papa and Papsey. | Father of Salome and Sheba, brother of Georgiana, and owner of one-half of Dandy Dick. | A clergyman who instructed Blore to back Dandy Dick on behalf of the Restoration Fund. | The Dean of St. Marvells, father of Salome and Sheba, who gave a mixture to Dandy and was involved in an incident with Noah Topping. | Dr. Jedd, the Dean of St. Marvells, who was mistaken for a culprit by Noah. | A character in the play, addressed as Augustin and Gus, who is a dean.</data>
      <data key="d6">["Dean of St. Marvell's, portrayed by Mr. John Clayton.","Father of Salome and Sheba, whom they plan to coax for money.","Father of Salome and Sheba, referred to as poor Papa.","The father of Miss Jedd, who is out of the way according to Blore.","Portly man of about fifty, father of Salome and Sheba.","A character in the play, father of Salome and Sheba, who invites Tarver and Darbey to dinner and is concerned about bills.","Father of Sheba and Salome, concerned about bills.","A character in the play, father of Sheba and Salome, concerned about household expenses and the Minister Spire restoration.","A clergyman and father of Salome and Sheba, brother of Georgiana Tidman.","Brother of Georgiana Tidman, widower, father of Salome and Sheba, and Dean of St. Marvells.","A character in the play, brother of Georgiana, referred to as Augustin or Gus.","A widower for fifteen years, brother of Georgiana, and host at the Deanery.","A widower of fifteen years, addressed as Jedd and Gus, who holds a position in St. Marvells.","A character in the play, referred to as Jedd, who is the brother of Georgiana and uncle of Salome and Sheba.","A character in the play, referred to as 'pa' by Sheba and Salome, and as 'Augustin' by Georgiana.","Father of Sheba and Salome, from whom they owe forty pounds unknown.","A character who is the father of Salome and Sheba, and has offered a thousand pounds to the Restoration Fund.","A character who has offered a thousand pounds to the Restoration Fund and is anxious for the post to bring today's Times.","A character who is worried about a thousand pounds to the Restoration Fund and bills, and later reads about seven wealthy people contributing.","Brother of Georgiana, a clergyman, concerned about propriety.","Father of Georgiana, Sheba, and Salome; reluctant to house Dandy Dick.","A character in the play who is upset with Mr. Darbey and later enters with Blore carrying a lantern.","A character who is the Dean, referred to as Jedd and Gus, and is unhappy about Sir Tristram staying.","A dean who is short of money and concerned about the church spire.","A clergyman who is horrified by Georgiana's proposal and concerned about the state of the Deanery.","Clergyman who gives Blore fifty pounds to bet on his horse Dandy Dick, despite earlier resisting temptation.","A character in the play who is upset and plans to administer a bolus to a horse in his stable.","The Dean of the Deanery, who is mixing a pudding and reminiscing about his youth.","A character who takes the basin and goes to the stable, wearing Sir Tristram's coat and cap.","The father of Salome and Sheba, mentioned by Sheba.","A person referred to by Blore as having been taken by the constable.","Master of Blore, referred to as the Dean.","A character who appears at the wicket with his head enveloped in a fur cap and is later released from the cell by Hannah.","A dean who is in trouble with the law and hiding from his family and community.","A dean facing a calamity of almost national importance, being taken to Durnstone.","A clergyman who is eating a joint prepared by Hannah.","A gentleman who has been accused by Noah of alienating Hannah's affections and embezzling his meal.","The Dean of St. Marvells, brother of Georgiana, who has mysteriously disappeared.","The Very Reverend Augustin Jedd, Dean of St. Marvells, missing person.","Man in handcuffs, accused of dosing Dandy Dick.","A man discovered administering a remedy to a horse, accused of trespassing and alienating Noah's wife's affections.","The Dean, who is rescued and gradually recovers.","A character in the play who is rescued and is concerned about funding the reward.","A character in the play, referred to as Papa by Sheba, who is concerned about the horse Dandy Dick and the money won towards the Spire.","Brother of Georgiana, who was hauled out of a cart by Sir Tristram and had a nose-bag put over his head.","Father of Salome and Sheba, referred to as Papa and Papsey.","Father of Salome and Sheba, brother of Georgiana, and owner of one-half of Dandy Dick.","A clergyman who instructed Blore to back Dandy Dick on behalf of the Restoration Fund.","The Dean of St. Marvells, father of Salome and Sheba, who gave a mixture to Dandy and was involved in an incident with Noah Topping.","Dr. Jedd, the Dean of St. Marvells, who was mistaken for a culprit by Noah.","A character in the play, addressed as Augustin and Gus, who is a dean."]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Augustin","Dean","Dr. Jedd","Gus","Jedd","Pa","Papa","Papsey","poor Papa","THE DEAN","The Dean","the Dean","the Dean of St. Marvells","The Very Rev. Augustin Jedd, D.D."]</data>
      <data key="d8">["dd_cast_c0001","dd_act01_c0002","dd_act01_c0003","dd_act01_c0004","dd_act01_c0005","dd_act01_c0006","dd_act01_c0007","dd_act01_c0008","dd_act01_c0009","dd_act01_c0010","dd_act01_c0011","dd_act01_c0012","dd_act01_c0013","dd_act01_c0014","dd_act01_c0015","dd_act01_c0017","dd_act02_c0002","dd_act02_c0003","dd_act02_c0004","dd_act02_c0005","dd_act02_c0006","dd_act02_c0007","dd_act02_c0008","dd_act02_c0009","dd_act02_c0010","dd_act02_c0011","dd_act02_c0012","dd_act02_c0013","dd_act02_c0014","dd_act02_c0016","dd_act02_c0017","dd_act03s01_c0004","dd_act03s01_c0005","dd_act03s01_c0006","dd_act03s01_c0007","dd_act03s01_c0008","dd_act03s01_c0009","dd_act03s01_c0010","dd_act03s01_c0011","dd_act03s01_c0012","dd_act03s01_c0013","dd_act03s02_c0003","dd_act03s02_c0004","dd_act03s02_c0005","dd_act03s02_c0006","dd_act03s02_c0007","dd_act03s02_c0008","dd_act03s02_c0009","dd_act03s02_c0010","dd_act03s02_c0011"]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="n_blore_bc324ff851ac">
      <data key="d3">Blore</data>
      <data key="d4">CHARACTER</data>
      <data key="d5">Butler at the Deanery, portrayed by Mr. Arthur Cecil. | The butler, a venerable-looking person with a clerical suggestion about his dress. | A servant who shows in visitors and is dismissed by Salome. | Servant of the Dean, carries books, flowers, and umbrella. | Servant in the Dean's household, brings the second post and comments on the girls crying. | A servant at the Deanery. | Servant who brings a telegram and announces Mrs. Tidman. | A character who enters to remove the tea-tray. | A servant who removes the tea-tray and brings the violin-case and music roll. | A character who enters with the paper and is hushed by the ladies. | A servant who announces the fire at the Swan Inn. | Servant at the Deanery. | A character who enters with the Dean carrying a lighted lantern. | A character who is a servant, carries a lantern, and is instructed to put Sir Tristram to bed. | The Dean's butler, who is revealed to have been at St. Marvells Races and invests servants' savings. | Servant at the Deanery who confesses to betting on horse races and accepts fifty pounds from the Dean to bet on Dandy Dick. | A character in the play who is leaving in a month and assists the Dean. | A servant at the Deanery who is concerned about the Dean's behavior and secretly poisons the pudding intended for Dandy Dick. | A servant who empties a packet of strychnine into a basin and stirs it, saying he has cooked Dandy Dick. | A person who exclaims that they have got the Dean. | A man from the Deanery, addressed as Mr. Blore and Edward. He appears with a troubled look and speaks mournfully. | A character in the play, referred to as Edward Blore, who is visiting Hannah and has brought cards from servants. | A character from the Deanery who visits Hannah and Noah Topping. | Servant from the Deanery, an old friend of Hannah. | A servant of the Dean who introduced a foreign element into the mixture. | Servant of the Dean who put the mixture on Bonny Betsy instead of Dandy.</data>
      <data key="d6">["Butler at the Deanery, portrayed by Mr. Arthur Cecil.","The butler, a venerable-looking person with a clerical suggestion about his dress.","A servant who shows in visitors and is dismissed by Salome.","Servant of the Dean, carries books, flowers, and umbrella.","Servant in the Dean's household, brings the second post and comments on the girls crying.","A servant at the Deanery.","Servant who brings a telegram and announces Mrs. Tidman.","A character who enters to remove the tea-tray.","A servant who removes the tea-tray and brings the violin-case and music roll.","A character who enters with the paper and is hushed by the ladies.","A servant who announces the fire at the Swan Inn.","Servant at the Deanery.","A character who enters with the Dean carrying a lighted lantern.","A character who is a servant, carries a lantern, and is instructed to put Sir Tristram to bed.","The Dean's butler, who is revealed to have been at St. Marvells Races and invests servants' savings.","Servant at the Deanery who confesses to betting on horse races and accepts fifty pounds from the Dean to bet on Dandy Dick.","A character in the play who is leaving in a month and assists the Dean.","A servant at the Deanery who is concerned about the Dean's behavior and secretly poisons the pudding intended for Dandy Dick.","A servant who empties a packet of strychnine into a basin and stirs it, saying he has cooked Dandy Dick.","A person who exclaims that they have got the Dean.","A man from the Deanery, addressed as Mr. Blore and Edward. He appears with a troubled look and speaks mournfully.","A character in the play, referred to as Edward Blore, who is visiting Hannah and has brought cards from servants.","A character from the Deanery who visits Hannah and Noah Topping.","Servant from the Deanery, an old friend of Hannah.","A servant of the Dean who introduced a foreign element into the mixture.","Servant of the Dean who put the mixture on Bonny Betsy instead of Dandy."]</data>
      <data key="d7">["BLORE","Edward","Edward Blore","Mr. Blore"]</data>
