    <node id="n_georgiana_tidman_9a9719259216">
      <data key="d3">Georgiana Tidman</data>
      <data key="d4">CHARACTER</data>
      <data key="d5">A widow, the Dean's sister, portrayed by Mrs. John Wood. | The Dean's widowed sister, coming to live with them. | Widow of a misguided husband, formerly known as 'the Daisy of the Turf', sister of the Dean, and aunt to Salome and Sheba. | A character in the play, sister of the Dean, formerly a racehorse owner under the name George Tidd. | The Dean's widowed sister, formerly a horse racer under the name George Tidd. | A character in the play, referred to as George and Mrs. Tidman, who is the sister of the Dean and mother of Salome and Sheba. | A character in the play, referred to as 'George' by Sir Tristram. | Aunt of Sheba and Salome, referred to as Aunt Georgiana and Aunt Tidman; she is involved with Dandy Dick and the Durnstone Handicap. | Aunt of Sheba and Salome, who knows about horse racing and initially refuses but then agrees to help them bet on Dandy Dick. | Aunt of Salome and Sheba, pouring tea after dinner. | A character who has a betting book and is working out figures related to Dandy Dick's feeding. | A character who reacts to the Dean's announcement and later reflects on the evening. | A character who comments on the English evening and has a betting book. | Sister of the Dean, referred to as George Tidd, concerned about the horse Dandy Dick. | Daughter of the Dean; suggests bringing Dandy Dick into the Deanery stables. | A character who is optimistic about the horse Dandy and interacts with Sir Tristram and Hatcham. | A character who is the Dean's sister, referred to as George, and arranges for Sir Tristram to stay in the nursery. | Sister of the Dean, referred to as 'little sister' and 'poor widowed sister'. | The Dean's sister, who urges him to bet on a horse and mentions Blore's familiarity with racecourses. | A character who enters pale and agitated, carrying an umbrella and a lighted candle, and says she has been dreaming. | A character in the play who is overtrained, emotional, and cares deeply for her share of the horse Dandy. | The aunt of Salome and Sheba, who scolds them. | Aunt of Salome and Sheba, who confronts Tarver and Darbey. | A lady who visits the police station with Sir Tristram, dressed for the race-course. | A lady visiting the police station, sister of Dr. Jedd, the Dean of St. Marvells. | Sister of Augustin Jedd, referred to as Gus's sister. | Sister of Gus, who is the Dean. | A woman who is disappointed in the Dean and asks about a whistle. | Aunt Georgiana, who enters excitedly and helps rescue the Dean. | A character in the play who recounts rescuing the Dean and winning money on the horse race. | A character in the play, sister of Augustin, who helped Dandy Dick by giving him whisky before the race. | Goes out at the window with Sir Tristram following Hatcham. | Sister of the Dean, aunt of Salome and Sheba, and owner of the other half of Dandy Dick. | Owner of the other half of Dandy Dick. | Present at the Deanery, tells Noah not to bring domestic affairs there. | A character who speaks to the Dean about his troubles and uses a horse metaphor. | A character in the play who speaks to the Dean and Sir Tristram.</data>
      <data key="d6">["A widow, the Dean's sister, portrayed by Mrs. John Wood.","The Dean's widowed sister, coming to live with them.","Widow of a misguided husband, formerly known as 'the Daisy of the Turf', sister of the Dean, and aunt to Salome and Sheba.","A character in the play, sister of the Dean, formerly a racehorse owner under the name George Tidd.","The Dean's widowed sister, formerly a horse racer under the name George Tidd.","A character in the play, referred to as George and Mrs. Tidman, who is the sister of the Dean and mother of Salome and Sheba.","A character in the play, referred to as 'George' by Sir Tristram.","Aunt of Sheba and Salome, referred to as Aunt Georgiana and Aunt Tidman; she is involved with Dandy Dick and the Durnstone Handicap.","Aunt of Sheba and Salome, who knows about horse racing and initially refuses but then agrees to help them bet on Dandy Dick.","Aunt of Salome and Sheba, pouring tea after dinner.","A character who has a betting book and is working out figures related to Dandy Dick's feeding.","A character who reacts to the Dean's announcement and later reflects on the evening.","A character who comments on the English evening and has a betting book.","Sister of the Dean, referred to as George Tidd, concerned about the horse Dandy Dick.","Daughter of the Dean; suggests bringing Dandy Dick into the Deanery stables.","A character who is optimistic about the horse Dandy and interacts with Sir Tristram and Hatcham.","A character who is the Dean's sister, referred to as George, and arranges for Sir Tristram to stay in the nursery.","Sister of the Dean, referred to as 'little sister' and 'poor widowed sister'.","The Dean's sister, who urges him to bet on a horse and mentions Blore's familiarity with racecourses.","A character who enters pale and agitated, carrying an umbrella and a lighted candle, and says she has been dreaming.","A character in the play who is overtrained, emotional, and cares deeply for her share of the horse Dandy.","The aunt of Salome and Sheba, who scolds them.","Aunt of Salome and Sheba, who confronts Tarver and Darbey.","A lady who visits the police station with Sir Tristram, dressed for the race-course.","A lady visiting the police station, sister of Dr. Jedd, the Dean of St. Marvells.","Sister of Augustin Jedd, referred to as Gus's sister.","Sister of Gus, who is the Dean.","A woman who is disappointed in the Dean and asks about a whistle.","Aunt Georgiana, who enters excitedly and helps rescue the Dean.","A character in the play who recounts rescuing the Dean and winning money on the horse race.","A character in the play, sister of Augustin, who helped Dandy Dick by giving him whisky before the race.","Goes out at the window with Sir Tristram following Hatcham.","Sister of the Dean, aunt of Salome and Sheba, and owner of the other half of Dandy Dick.","Owner of the other half of Dandy Dick.","Present at the Deanery, tells Noah not to bring domestic affairs there.","A character who speaks to the Dean about his troubles and uses a horse metaphor.","A character in the play who speaks to the Dean and Sir Tristram."]</data>
      <data key="d7">["Aunt George","Aunt Georgiana","Aunt Tidman","George","George Tidd","GEORGIANA","Georgiana","Mrs. Tidman","Tidd"]</data>
